\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae7fdbe5-ac31-5cc5-8e79-33235643a822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486427 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 10-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x={c}_{1}+4{c}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186486345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ to $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd3d1f8f-1ec9-5277-bd94-fc774bb919de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486444 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486482 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50c856b2-5de1-5fc6-b8b6-216f5affc71d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486482 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 175921